From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sat, 17 Oct 2026 10:56:30 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] textures: decode palette and swizzled 16-bit textures in one
 pass, with a test against a plain reference

decode_texture() used to copy the whole texture into a buffer (unswizzling on the way) and then walk it again for the palette
lookup and the channel expansion. Now:
- decode_indexed_fused() reads palette textures (CLUT4/8, linear or swizzled) and swizzled 16-bit textures straight from guest
  memory, a 16x8-byte block at a time, and writes RGBA8 once.
- The 16-bit formats expand through 32- and 64-entry channel tables instead of a multiply and a divide per channel.
- NO_FUSED_DECODE=1 goes back to the general path; NO_FAST_TEXTURE_DECODE=1 and CHECK_TEXTURE_DECODE=1 are unchanged.
- tests/texture_decode_tests.cpp compares every format (linear and swizzled, widths and heights that do and do not fill the
  swizzle blocks, buffers wider than the texture, palette masks, shifts, offsets) with a reference written from the format rules:
  1500 random textures, bit for bit.

Verified on x86-64: the new test passes; palette textures decode about 2.8 times faster in a micro-benchmark. Not verified: the
gain on the PS Vita (a level load uploads a hundred-odd textures at 5-24 ms each there; nothing has run this build on the console).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

